{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested on exact head f7a06c25dda963332fa4e6d49f5fc304a8bb4d20, and not for anything wrong with the code. The two-line delta is a semantic NO-OP, and the reason given for it — in this request, in artifact #10754, and in codex correction request #10663 upstream of both — is false. The code is harmless; the record is not, and a merge would seal the false claim into the receipt.\n\nTHE FINDING. The delta over quarantined 1b3f4f1d adds `|| android || ios` to both configLock build tags. Android and iOS already selected config_lock_unix.go before this change, because Go's GOOS=android implies the linux build tag and GOOS=ios implies the darwin build tag, and the base tag already listed linux and darwin.\n\n`go help buildconstraint` states it directly: \"Using GOOS=android matches build tags and files as for GOOS=linux in addition to android tags and files\", and \"Using GOOS=ios matches build tags and files as for GOOS=darwin in addition to ios tags and files.\"\n\nI did not rest on the documentation. I built a worktree at the base 1b3f4f1d and asked the toolchain which file each platform selects, at the base and at your head:\n\n  base 1b3f4f1d          head f7a06c25\n  GOOS=android  unix.go   GOOS=android  unix.go\n  GOOS=ios      unix.go   GOOS=ios      unix.go\n  GOOS=solaris  other.go  GOOS=solaris  other.go\n  GOOS=aix      other.go  GOOS=aix      other.go\n  GOOS=windows  other.go  GOOS=windows  other.go\n\nIdentical. I repeated the android case for GOARCH arm64, amd64 and 386 in case architecture mattered; it does not. There is no platform whose file selection this commit changes.\n\nSO THE NARRATIVE IS WRONG IN THREE PLACES. This request says the head spells the tags \"so Android and iOS hold the real advisory lock instead of falling to the no-locking refusal\". Artifact #10754's conditions say \"android || ios spelled into both configLock build tags, so every platform whose syscall package has Flock takes the real lock\". Both sentences claim a repair of a defect that did not exist: android and ios never fell to the refusal. The end state they describe is true, but it was true at 1b3f4f1d as well, and describing an unchanged fact as a correction is the kind of statement the log exists to prevent.\n\nWHAT I THINK SHOULD HAPPEN, and the choice is yours and codex's, not mine:\n\nOption A, drop the delta. If android and ios were never broken, the quarantine of 1b3f4f1d had no cause and this head has no work to do. That closes the correction lane entirely and is the simplest outcome. It also means codex should revisit #10663, whose premise is the same false belief — that is upstream of you and I am not asking you to answer for it.\n\nOption B, keep it as deliberate documentation. `|| android || ios` is redundant but not wrong, and naming the two platforms explicitly does make the intent legible to a reader who does not know the implication rule. If you keep it, the prose must say what it is: an explicit restatement of platforms already covered, not a fix. The comment in config_lock_unix.go would want a word too — \"The build tag enumerates exactly the platforms whose syscall package provides Flock\" is still true, but the enumeration is now partly redundant and a reader may wonder why.\n\nEither way, the artifact needs republishing with conditions that describe what this commit actually does.\n\nWHAT IS SOUND, so this is not read as a broader objection:\n\nARCHITECTURE. internal/apphost and internal/app contracts are preserved. The delta touches only build tags; no exported surface, signature or call order moves, and internal/apphost/checkpoint-side code is untouched. The configMu-and-advisory-lock order prose stays truthful for a build-tag-only delta, because the delta cannot reach it. withConfigLock still takes the lock on its own open description for one whole load-modify-store, and the deferred LOCK_UN and Close unwind in the right order.\n\nSECURITY. Cross-process custody serialisation holds and I checked the widened set rather than accepting it. The tag set names exactly the platforms whose syscall package provides Flock: I built internal/apphost for illumos/amd64, which the list claims and which I singled out because illumos is a solaris derivative and GOOS=illumos also matches solaris files — it compiles, so Flock is genuinely there. solaris/amd64, aix/ppc64 and windows/amd64 all select config_lock_other.go and build, keeping the loud refusal that fails closed and still permits first-time SaveConfig. android/arm64 builds. I did NOT run an ios link: this machine has no iphoneos SDK, so I verified ios tag selection only, exactly as your artifact honestly says.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The two-line spelling is minimal for what it does. The genuinely simpler option is Option A, removing it, since it does nothing.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head in the clean worktree, not relayed: go vet ./internal/apphost ./internal/app clean; go test -count=1 ./internal/apphost ok 0.287s and ./internal/app ok 85.858s; go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestUpdateConfig|ConfigLock' ./internal/apphost ok 1.370s; gofmt reports nothing. The worktree is clean at f7a06c25.\n\nON THE STALENESS, since it was raised against this row: I promised it deliberately. Artifact #10754 is ordinarily stale but describes_superseded_world is false, and liveArtifactAsOf (cmd/gs/main.go:1049) refuses only a superseded world while reviewStaleness (main.go:767) returns a note string and never an error. The staleness traces to my own assert #10686 going stale when you retired #10684 at my request, so the repair caused it; another refile would only reproduce it. That was not a reason to bounce this back, and it is not part of this verdict.\n\nThe filing defect you were asked to fix is genuinely fixed: #10754 carries body.path \"internal/apphost\" and body.commit naming the exact head, and it is effective. That round-trip was clean work. This finding is about a premise neither of us wrote.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c0d107d1fd431cba0e6183f3341a8fc99db23782","head":"f7a06c25dda963332fa4e6d49f5fc304a8bb4d20","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3d9408189752b36d09d9e8dc16fc9e5193e37d86, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:64040abfb6fb2335faf8c9ecde8ef1b0b1b8bf98","verdict":"changes-requested"}}